1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,019
ChatGPT once made up court

2
00:00:02,020 --> 00:00:02,420
cases.

3
00:00:03,160 --> 00:00:03,540
Not one.

4
00:00:04,140 --> 00:00:04,920
A whole list.

5
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:07,359
And a real lawyer handed them to

6
00:00:07,360 --> 00:00:08,040
a real judge.

7
00:00:08,880 --> 00:00:10,100
The judge looked them up.

8
00:00:10,640 --> 00:00:11,500
They didn't exist.

9
00:00:12,220 --> 00:00:13,659
And when the lawyer asked it, is

10
00:00:13,660 --> 00:00:15,600
this case real, it said yes.

11
00:00:16,560 --> 00:00:18,019
Last time, we saw that AI

12
00:00:18,020 --> 00:00:19,939
guesses what sounds right, not

13
00:00:19,940 --> 00:00:20,460
what is right.

14
00:00:21,020 --> 00:00:22,360
Here, those two split apart.

15
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:24,240
Why would it do that?

16
00:00:25,100 --> 00:00:26,240
Let's un-jargon it.

17
00:00:28,033 --> 00:00:28,833
It wasn't lying.

18
00:00:29,693 --> 00:00:30,973
Lying means you know the truth.

19
00:00:31,553 --> 00:00:33,232
It was guessing, like a student

20
00:00:33,233 --> 00:00:34,853
bluffing in a closed book exam.

21
00:00:35,713 --> 00:00:37,212
When an AI makes something up

22
00:00:37,213 --> 00:00:39,032
and says it like a fact, that's

23
00:00:39,033 --> 00:00:40,093
called a hallucination.

24
00:00:40,773 --> 00:00:42,052
Odd word, because it isn't

25
00:00:42,053 --> 00:00:42,693
seeing things.

26
00:00:43,273 --> 00:00:44,013
It's bluffing.

27
00:00:44,913 --> 00:00:46,552
Picture that student stuck on a

28
00:00:46,553 --> 00:00:46,913
question.

29
00:00:47,553 --> 00:00:48,992
Leave it blank and that's zero

30
00:00:48,993 --> 00:00:49,453
points.

31
00:00:50,053 --> 00:00:51,112
Write something that sounds

32
00:00:51,113 --> 00:00:52,852
right and maybe it gets marked

33
00:00:52,853 --> 00:00:53,253
correct.

34
00:00:54,173 --> 00:00:55,133
So three questions.

35
00:00:55,633 --> 00:00:57,372
Why does AI make things up at

36
00:00:57,373 --> 00:00:57,613
all?

37
00:00:58,293 --> 00:00:59,413
Why does it sound so sure?

38
00:01:00,193 --> 00:01:01,353
And how do you protect yourself?

39
00:01:02,167 --> 00:01:04,426
Question one, why make things up

40
00:01:04,427 --> 00:01:04,867
at all?

41
00:01:05,527 --> 00:01:06,967
Because of how it gets marked.

42
00:01:07,547 --> 00:01:09,966
In 2025, researchers at

43
00:01:09,967 --> 00:01:12,326
OpenAI, the company behind

44
00:01:12,327 --> 00:01:14,586
ChatGPT, published a paper

45
00:01:14,587 --> 00:01:16,186
called Why Language Models

46
00:01:16,187 --> 00:01:16,787
Hallucinate.

47
00:01:17,387 --> 00:01:18,747
Their answer is that exam.

48
00:01:19,507 --> 00:01:21,327
When AIs are trained and tested,

49
00:01:21,787 --> 00:01:23,166
most of the scoring works the

50
00:01:23,167 --> 00:01:23,687
same way.

51
00:01:24,107 --> 00:01:25,627
A right answer gets a point.

52
00:01:26,247 --> 00:01:27,587
A wrong answer gets zero.

53
00:01:28,167 --> 00:01:29,546
And I don't know, also gets

54
00:01:29,547 --> 00:01:29,947
zero.

55
00:01:30,447 --> 00:01:31,987
So bluffing always wins.

56
00:01:32,887 --> 00:01:33,966
And some facts it has barely

57
00:01:33,967 --> 00:01:34,387
seen.

58
00:01:34,947 --> 00:01:37,066
The researchers asked one AI for

59
00:01:37,067 --> 00:01:38,726
one of their birthdays and told

60
00:01:38,727 --> 00:01:40,327
it to answer only if it knew.

61
00:01:40,887 --> 00:01:42,886
Three tries, three different

62
00:01:42,887 --> 00:01:44,607
dates, all wrong.

63
00:01:48,667 --> 00:01:50,706
Question two, why does it sound

64
00:01:50,707 --> 00:01:51,247
so sure?

65
00:01:52,527 --> 00:01:53,566
Remember, it's always guessing

66
00:01:53,567 --> 00:01:54,467
what sounds right.

67
00:01:55,007 --> 00:01:56,626
A real fact and a made up one

68
00:01:56,627 --> 00:01:58,386
come out of the same machine in

69
00:01:58,387 --> 00:02:00,367
the same smooth, friendly voice.

70
00:02:00,967 --> 00:02:02,146
There's no wobble when it's

71
00:02:02,147 --> 00:02:02,447
wrong.

72
00:02:03,207 --> 00:02:04,666
And its training to be a helpful

73
00:02:04,667 --> 00:02:06,126
assistant was marked the same

74
00:02:06,127 --> 00:02:06,467
way.

75
00:02:06,887 --> 00:02:08,606
A sure answer beats, I'm not

76
00:02:08,607 --> 00:02:08,907
sure.

77
00:02:09,607 --> 00:02:11,326
That's how an airline's chatbot

78
00:02:11,327 --> 00:02:13,286
in Canada told a man whose

79
00:02:13,287 --> 00:02:15,306
grandmother had just died that

80
00:02:15,307 --> 00:02:16,946
he could buy a full price ticket

81
00:02:16,947 --> 00:02:18,726
and claim a family funeral

82
00:02:18,727 --> 00:02:19,627
discount later.

83
00:02:20,227 --> 00:02:21,726
The airline's own website said

84
00:02:21,727 --> 00:02:22,207
the opposite.

85
00:02:23,127 --> 00:02:25,486
In 2024, a tribunal made the

86
00:02:25,487 --> 00:02:26,267
airline pay.

87
00:02:26,787 --> 00:02:28,047
Its chatbot, its mistake.

88
00:02:28,867 --> 00:02:30,706
Question three, how do you

89
00:02:30,707 --> 00:02:31,467
protect yourself?

90
00:02:32,147 --> 00:02:32,727
Three habits.

91
00:02:33,687 --> 00:02:35,686
One, ask for sources and then

92
00:02:35,687 --> 00:02:36,727
actually open them.

93
00:02:37,207 --> 00:02:38,107
Remember the lawyer.

94
00:02:38,707 --> 00:02:40,326
He asked for cases and got

95
00:02:40,327 --> 00:02:40,767
cases.

96
00:02:41,367 --> 00:02:42,487
They just weren't real.

97
00:02:43,127 --> 00:02:44,106
A link you haven't clicked

98
00:02:44,107 --> 00:02:44,847
proves nothing.

99
00:02:45,567 --> 00:02:47,467
Two, make it an open book exam.

100
00:02:48,147 --> 00:02:49,666
Paste in the real thing, the

101
00:02:49,667 --> 00:02:51,347
letter, the web page, the rules,

102
00:02:51,667 --> 00:02:53,286
and ask it to answer only from

103
00:02:53,287 --> 00:02:53,567
that.

104
00:02:54,087 --> 00:02:55,766
It guesses far less when the

105
00:02:55,767 --> 00:02:57,266
answer is right in front of it.

106
00:02:57,267 --> 00:02:59,567
Far less, not never.

107
00:03:00,507 --> 00:03:02,086
Three, double check the details

108
00:03:02,087 --> 00:03:03,327
that are easiest to bluff.

109
00:03:04,327 --> 00:03:05,426
Names, numbers, dates, and

110
00:03:05,427 --> 00:03:05,647
quotes.

111
00:03:06,367 --> 00:03:08,167
So why does AI hallucinate?

112
00:03:08,907 --> 00:03:10,506
It's a guesser, marked like a

113
00:03:10,507 --> 00:03:12,146
student in an exam where a blank

114
00:03:12,147 --> 00:03:12,967
scores zero.

115
00:03:13,507 --> 00:03:14,147
So it bluffs.

116
00:03:14,807 --> 00:03:16,706
And right answers and wrong ones

117
00:03:16,707 --> 00:03:18,186
come out in the same confident

118
00:03:18,187 --> 00:03:18,647
voice.

119
00:03:19,107 --> 00:03:20,046
So you can't hear the

120
00:03:20,047 --> 00:03:20,427
difference.

121
00:03:21,387 --> 00:03:23,026
Sounding sure is not the same as

122
00:03:23,027 --> 00:03:23,527
being right.

123
00:03:24,247 --> 00:03:25,786
So treat it like a very fast

124
00:03:25,787 --> 00:03:27,247
student, not an expert.

125
00:03:27,827 --> 00:03:29,786
Give it the book and check its

126
00:03:29,787 --> 00:03:30,047
work.

127
00:03:30,767 --> 00:03:32,607
But so far, AI only talks.

128
00:03:33,267 --> 00:03:34,806
What happens when it can act and

129
00:03:34,807 --> 00:03:36,407
book the flight, send the email,

130
00:03:36,587 --> 00:03:37,187
spend the money?

131
00:03:37,727 --> 00:03:39,426
That's called an AI agent and

132
00:03:39,427 --> 00:03:39,907
it's next.

133
00:03:41,267 --> 00:03:42,267
Subscribe so you don't miss it.
